The Debye-Hückel screening length $\kappa^{-1}$ in an electrolyte solution is given by the formula:
$$\kappa^{-1} = \sqrt{\frac{\epsilon_r \epsilon_0 k_B T}{2 N_A e^2 I}}$$
where:
*   $\epsilon_r$ is the relative permittivity (dielectric constant) of the solvent (usually water, $\epsilon_r \approx 80$).
*   $\epsilon_0$ is the permittivity of free space ($8.854 \times 10^{-12} \text{ F/m}$).
*   $k_B$ is the Boltzmann constant ($1.381 \times 10^{-23} \text{ J/K}$).
*   $T$ is the absolute temperature ($298 \text{ K}$).
*   $N_A$ is Avogadro's number ($6.022 \times 10^{23} \text{ mol}^{-1}$).
*   $e$ is the elementary charge ($1.602 \times 10^{-19} \text{ C}$).
*   $I$ is the ionic strength.

